    "id": "lem:observable-reduction",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\((Q,P)\\in\\mathcal M_{L,\\eta}\\), the shared bridge satisfies \\(A_Pg=b_P\\) and the causal target satisfies \\(\\theta=R_0g\\). Consequently, \\(H(P)\\ne\\varnothing\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Q",
      "P",
      "M_L_eta",
      "L",
      "eta",
      "g",
      "A_P",
      "b_P",
      "theta",
      "R_0",
      "H_P"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "def:bridge-fiber",
      "ass:consistency",
      "ass:balanced-randomization",
      "ass:common-proxy-kernel",
      "ass:proxy-exclusion",
      "ass:shared-contrast-bridge"
    ],
    "justification": "This is the observable reduction that separates bridge algebra from the harder causal necessity direction.",
    "gap": "Su et al. derive the two-trial bridge formula; the stacked multigraph operator and target map are not stated there.",
    "consumer": "It gives network analysts a finite linear system whose solutions encode every causally admissible root contrast.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix a trial \\(e\\), a covariate value \\(x\\), and a proxy value \\(z\\).  The conditioning event has positive probability by observed-cell positivity.  Put \\(\\bar g_e(w,x)=g_{r_e}(w,x)-g_{l_e}(w,x)\\).  Balanced randomization and consistency give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_Q[\\widetilde Y_e\\mid Z_e=z,X=x,S=e]\n&=\\mathbb E_Q[Y(r_e)-Y(l_e)\\mid Z_e=z,X=x,S=e].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIndeed, conditional on all baseline variables and \\(Y_a\\), each treatment arm has probability \\(1/2\\), and the factor \\(2\\) in \\(\\widetilde Y_e\\) cancels that probability.  Let \\(H^Q_{e,x}[z,u]=Q(U=u\\mid Z_e=z,X=x,S=e)\\).  Successive conditioning, proxy exclusion, and the shared-contrast bridge imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_Q[Y(r_e)-Y(l_e)\\mid z,x,e]\n&=\\sum_u H^Q_{e,x}[z,u]\n  \\mathbb E_Q[Y(r_e)-Y(l_e)\\mid x,u,e]\\\\\n&=\\sum_{u,w}H^Q_{e,x}[z,u]C_x(w\\mid u)\\bar g_e(w,x).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nCommon-proxy invariance and proxy exclusion also give, entry by entry,\n\\[\nT_{e,x}[z,w]=\\sum_u H^Q_{e,x}[z,u]C_x(w\\mid u).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_Q[\\widetilde Y_e\\mid z,x,e]\n=\\sum_wT_{e,x}[z,w]\\bar g_e(w,x),\n\\]\nwhich is precisely the \\((e,x,z)\\)-row of \\(A_Pg=b_P\\), by the definition of the reduced incidence row \\(B_e\\).  Stacking proves \\(A_Pg=b_P\\).\n\nFor the target population, the tower property and the shared-contrast bridge give, for \\(a=1,\\ldots,k\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta_a\n&=\\sum_{x,u}Q(x,u\\mid S=0)\n  \\mathbb E_Q[Y(a)-Y(0)\\mid x,u,S=0]\\\\\n&=\\sum_{x,u,w}Q(x,u\\mid S=0)C_x(w\\mid u)g_a(w,x)\\\\\n&=\\sum_{x,w}P(X=x,W=w\\mid S=0)g_a(w,x)\n=(R_0g)_a,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere the penultimate equality uses the common \\(W\\)-given-\\((X,U)\\) kernel.  Thus \\(\\theta=R_0g\\).  Since the model assumption supplies this \\(g\\), it is an element of \\(H(P)\\), and \\(H(P)\\ne\\varnothing\\).",
